  "slug": "age-framework-b5f8",
  "summary": "Physics-informed and martingale-based frameworks now target stochastic rhythm transmission, phase response inference, and first-passage timing in gene…",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is inference and analysis of stochastic dynamical models of gene expression and signaling, with bursty production and degradation as recurring ingredients. The arXiv martingale framework links degradation, bursting and cycle variability to rhythm transmission and is fitted to p53 data. The biorxiv PINN-iPRC preprint infers infinitesimal phase response curves from finite-pulse circadian recordings and is validated on synthetic data. A second preprint treats first-passage-time variability under bursty production and degradation through optimal feedback. A fourth examines how steady-state dose-response curves shape information transmission in single- and multi-step signaling. What is new is the shift toward physics-informed and analytic frameworks that extract model-level quantities, such as phase response or transmission properties, directly from dynamics rather than introducing new model classes. All four items are preprints without published validation, and the set is heterogeneous in scope.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-30"
}
